Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how you can use the **`NodeIdConverter_format`** helper to tack the node‑ID onto an existing `StringBuilder` that you’re building up for a log entry.

> **Assumptions**
> * You’re working in a Java 8 (or later) environment that has the `functions` namespace available.
> * `logEvent` is an instance of `org.elasticsearch.common.logging.LogEvent` (or any other class that represents a log event in your code).
> * The helper will mutate the `StringBuilder` you pass in – it simply appends the node‑ID if one can be extracted from the event.

```java
import static functions.NodeIdConverter_format;   // <-- make the helper available

public class LogUtil {

    /**
     * Appends the node‑ID (if present) to the supplied StringBuilder.
     *
     * @param logEvent  the LogEvent from which to extract the node‑ID
     * @param logBuilder the StringBuilder that already contains the rest of the log line
     */
    public static void appendNodeId(LogEvent logEvent, StringBuilder logBuilder) {
        // The helper expects the arguments as plain objects – it will internally
        // convert them to the appropriate Java types.  We simply forward the
        // objects themselves; no need to call toString() on them.
        NodeIdConverter_format(new NodeIdConverter_format.Params(
                /* event */      logEvent,
                /* toAppendTo */ logBuilder
        ));
    }

    // Example usage
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1. Build the rest of the log line first
        StringBuilder logBuilder = new StringBuilder();
        logBuilder.append("timestamp=2026-07-08T12:34:56Z ");
        logBuilder.append("level=INFO ");
        logBuilder.append("message=\"User logged in\" ");

        // 2. Obtain the LogEvent (in real code this comes from Elasticsearch)
        LogEvent logEvent = getLogEventFromElasticsearch();

        // 3. Append the node‑ID
        appendNodeId(logEvent, logBuilder);

        // 4. Emit the final log line
        System.out.println(logBuilder.toString());
    }

    // Dummy method – replace with your actual code that fetches a LogEvent
    private static LogEvent getLogEventFromElasticsearch() {
        // In practice this would be something like:
        // return client.getLogEvent(...);
        return new LogEvent() {
            @Override
            public String getMessage() { return "User logged in"; }
            @Override
            public String getLevel() { return "INFO"; }
            @Override
            public String getTimestamp() { return "2026-07-08T12:34:56Z"; }
            @Override
            public String getNodeId() { return "node-42"; }   // <-- node‑ID
        };
    }
}
```

### What’s happening under the hood?

1. **`NodeIdConverter_format`** receives two arguments:
   * `event` – the `LogEvent` instance.
   * `toAppendTo` – the `StringBuilder` you want to mutate.

2. Inside the helper (not shown here), the code will:
   * Inspect the `event` for a node‑ID (e.g., via `event.getNodeId()` or a similar accessor).
   * If a node‑ID is found, it will call `toAppendTo.append(nodeId)`.

3. After the call, your original `logBuilder` will contain the node‑ID appended to the end of the string you already built.

### Quick sanity check

If you run the `main` method above, you should see output similar to:

```
timestamp=2026-07-08T12:34:56Z level=INFO message="User logged in" node-42
```

(Your exact format may differ depending on how you build the initial string.)

Feel free to adapt the helper call to match the exact signatures your environment expects—if the helper truly requires *string* representations, just pass `logEvent.toString()` and `logBuilder.toString()` instead, but the mutation semantics will be the same.

